On the subject of making a patch to show statues with a monster letter
rather than a backtick.

Janis Papanagnou wrote:
>Are the new statues grey colored or do they have the color of the
>monster? I think the first one would be fine.

The color of statues is indeed grey, not the color of a live monster.
That's what makes the patch so beautiful:  you instantly recognize that
you are looking at a statue of a nypmh, or a statue of a lich, etc., as
soon as you enter the room.  It also makes statue traps more exciting,
when the letter suddenly takes color and attacks you.

P.D.Research wrote:
PDR>  The details of level.objects are typically hidden behind macros.

Thanks, I'll change that part.

PDR> Hallucinated objects which look like statues will end up
PDR> as "`", unless the item happens to actually be a statue, in
PDR> which case you'll be displaying it with accurate monster form.

Mimics that mimic statues also show up as backticks.  In any case,
whenever you think you see a statue but there isn't one, the game
treats it as a giant ant (mon[0]).  So that is easy to deal with -- if
there isn't actually a statue at the location, use mon[0], unless you
are hallucinating, in which case use random_monster().  I'll upload a
better patch later today.  The first patch, like I said when I posted
it, was just a proof of concept.

(Actually, hallucinated statues are always 'i' on my computer.  I have
no idea why. That's what's holding up the patch.)

Is there any other situation in which a statue glyph is attached to a
location except these three?

1) There really is a statue there
2) There is a mimic that has mimicked a statue
3)  You are hallucinating

PDR> But mapglyph() shouldn't delve into game play details like
PDR> Hallucination so fixing this would need to be done some other
PDR> way (such as using a full set of statue glyphs so that the higher
PDR> level  code can specify what [possibly false] type of statue to
PDR> display... :-).

You're saying that that a hallucinated fake statue might return to its
true form when you stop hallucinating?  My testing shows that the
update doesn't actually occur in tty mode; until you can see the object
again, the map isn't updated, so the old symbol remains.  Since the
glyphs are never changed, the patch has absolutely no effect in tiled
versions of nethack (that is, all vanilla behavior continues).

The old statue patch by M. Ryan did exactly what you suggest, by
introducing a large number of new glyphs.  The problem with that is
that it breaks save file compatibility and it breaks the non-tty
versions of the game (unless you modify the tilesets etc).
Putting the logic in mapglyph.c is harmless and clean.   And it lets
you use Hearse.

The code I am adding to mapglyph.c is read-only.  I may need to check
whether there is a statue at a location, or if you are hallucinating,
etc.  But I never make any changes to any internal structures.   My
reading of the code says mapglyph.c can return any letter it wants, and
no other part of nethack will ever complain.

I got the idea of using mapglyph.c from the comments at the top of
display.c.
>> Display decides _what_ is displayed at a given location.
>>  Drawing decides _how_ to draw a monster, fountain, sword, etc.

I think that what I am doing falls under drawing (i.e. mapglyph.c).  I
am choosing how to display the glyph of a statue in tty mode.  I am not
changing what is at the location of the statue.


